# Copy to .env.test at the project root and adjust for your test server.
#
# Used by the e2e suite only: web/playwright.config.js reads `.env.test` and
# injects it into the environment of the server under test. It holds the
# **app-level** keys only — the same set as .env.example: the question set, the
# debug switch and the verifier block.
#
# The model connection is not here. Since the workspace settings split it lives
# in the working location's reserved folder, and the suite has its own one of
# those: `.nojae/nojae-test-settings.json`, hand-authored, holding the server,
# key, model id, prompt, sampling, the vision flag, `lspEnabled` and
# `maxToolIteration`. The Playwright config switches the server under test onto
# that file and refuses to start the suite when it is missing. See the
# "Setting up tests for benchmarkit" section of the README.
#
# Precedence: the parsed values are applied last when the server is spawned,
# so they beat both the developer's .env and any key already exported in the
# calling environment — which matters, because the app exports its own settings
# into os.environ on every save. Point the suite at a different file with
# `E2E_ENV_FILE=other.test npm run test:e2e` instead of editing .env.test.
#
# A key left commented out is exported as *empty*, i.e. deliberately cleared
# rather than inherited — so this file, and nothing ambient, decides what a
# spec talks to.
#
# `.env.test` is gitignored like `.env`: it names a machine-local verifier and
# may carry its key. Keep this template secret-free.

# Benchmark question set (resources/<name>.json). `teste2e` is the 30-question
# set the benchmark specs are written against — the slowest thing a spec can
# wait on, so a smaller fixture set would not help here.
QUESTION_SET=teste2e
#LLM_DEBUG=1

# Verifier agent: pointed at the same test server, so a graded answer is
# possible. Without a verifier an answer cannot be graded and is failed, which
# would make every benchmark spec measure the wrong thing.
VERIFIER_AGENT_SYSPROMPT="You are a response expert analyser. The user will provide a json file containing the following structure { \"question\": \"<the_question>\", \"response\": \"<the_llm_response>\", \"expected\": \"<the_expected_answer_from_questions_file>\" }. Your goal is to evaluate if the response matches the expected answer. The question is provided for clarity only and you must not answer it. You must not think of what could be the response but only use the **expected** attribute value witch is the expected response. Words can differ but the meaning of both (response, expected) must match. If you think there is a match just respond 'true'. If not just respond 'false'. Do not add any other output or comments. Very important: Your response output must only contain one word: 'true' or 'false'."
VERIFIER_AGENT_SERVERURL=http://192.168.1.3:8080/v1
VERIFIER_AGENT_SERVERURL_API_KEY=not-needed
VERIFIER_AGENT_ENDPOINT=qwen3.8-flash-next-iq3_xxs

# MCP connectors and language servers are switched off for the suite by
# BENCHMARKIT_DISABLE_MCP / BENCHMARKIT_DISABLE_LSP, which the Playwright config
# exports; a spec that wants them starts its own server.
